{"kind":"request","text":"Planner: please independently review exact clean head 6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4 on branch request/serve-drop-acknowledge-flag, worktree /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/serve-drop-ack. Cut from main 0ec3618f; it fast-forwards.\n\nThis answers your request d255ce8c under my promise dd67c9f7. Three artifacts to co-sign, all at this head: c654df30 at cmd/gs, a69ea74d at docs, 551b3d13 at README.md. Please name all three with --artifact; a verdict naming fewer would bound the merge receipt to fewer paths and strand the rest.\n\nWHAT IT DOES. Deletes the --acknowledge-trusted-processes flag and its refusal from gs serve. Four deleted lines in cmd/gs/main.go and nothing else in the code.\n\nWHAT THIS REVERSES, AND PLEASE DO NOT REQUEST CHANGES ON THAT GROUND. Condition 1 of request 00279a9c required a per-invocation operator acknowledgement that fails closed when absent, implementing the supported-host posture adopted in decision 8a758fb7 under Hugh's adoption bc758b7a. This withdraws the per-invocation half and nothing else. Contradicting that condition is the point, and the operator who set it is the one withdrawing it. The loopback-only listener resolution, the resident-minted credentials, the Host and browser provenance checks and the rest of the posture are untouched. I have named those records rather than rested on them, so retiring any of them cannot stale this work.\n\nWHAT I VERIFIED MYSELF RATHER THAN TAKING FROM THE REQUEST. The flag had no consumer. The identifier acknowledgeTrustedProcesses occurred exactly twice in the whole tree, at its declaration and at the refusal reading it. It was a function-local *bool inside serveCommand that never escaped — not stored in a field, closure or package variable, never passed as an argument — and the FlagSet holding it is created per call by flags() and never escapes either, and no non-test Go file anywhere calls Lookup, Visit or VisitAll on a flag set. So nothing could read it by value or by name. It granted no authority and withheld none.\n\nWHAT I MOST WANT CHECKED, because this is the part that could be wrong in a way that matters.\n\nFirst, the security disclosure. Before this head the string \"trusted processes only\" appeared nowhere under docs/; the prose lived only inside the flag's own description and the pages explaining why the flag was required. So the risk in this change was never the deletion — it was deleting the boundary statement along with it. Three pages now state the boundary directly: docs/reference/gs/serve.md under Loopback only, docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md under Loopback only and why, and docs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md. Please read that prose as a security disclosure, not as documentation tidying, and say whether a reader now learns at least as much as before. I believe they learn more, because the boundary was previously framed as the justification for a flag, which invites reading the flag as the protection. If you disagree, that is a changes-requested.\n\nSecond, an out-of-scope change I made deliberately. docs/reference/architecture.md:181 asserted that gs serve \"requires an explicit per-invocation acknowledgement\", which the removal makes false, and AGENTS.md requires the architecture page to match the head. That clause now says the service discloses the posture on every start. Nothing else on the page moved and I judge no layer contract changed. Confirm that judgement, and say if you think the edit exceeds what the head forces.\n\nThird, that nothing load-bearing went with the flag. service.TrustedProcessPosture at internal/service/server.go:39 is unchanged, still printed unconditionally at cmd/gs/main.go:1716, still carried as TrustBoundary at server.go:177 and :251, with server_test.go:229 and :269 passing. Please confirm from the code rather than from this text.\n\nFourth, the test treatment. TestServeRequiresExplicitTrustedProcessAcknowledgement is deleted outright rather than inverted, on the reasoning that with no refusal left an inverted version would only assert that Go's flag package parses arguments. Seven call sites drop the argument with no assertion, timeout or control-flow change. Say if you think deleting rather than inverting loses coverage that should have been kept.\n\nA COMPATIBILITY BREAK YOU SHOULD WEIGH DELIBERATELY, recorded in full in assert cead45a5. This is not backward compatible. The resident running on this machine right now was launched with the flag in its command line, and Go's flag package refuses an unknown flag, so that exact invocation will fail to start after the rebuild — and every gitseq write here goes through that resident. I considered accepting the flag as a tolerated no-op and rejected it: that would leave a vestigial flag in the surface, which is the ceremony the request exists to delete, and condition 3 forbids it explicitly. If you think the break should be softened despite that, say so; it is a legitimate disagreement and I would rather have it in a verdict than after a failed restart.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD, after go clean -testcache, for you to re-run rather than trust: make vet exit 0; make build exit 0; make docs ok in 55.9s; make test exit 0, 28 packages ok, zero failures; make race exit 0, same 28 packages, nothing cached, zero races; gofmt -l . empty; git status --porcelain empty. The condition-3 gate passes: grep -rn 'acknowledge-trusted-processes' returns nothing anywhere in the worktree including .git. I also confirmed at the BASE commit, not only after the deletions, that public_surface_test.go, layout_test.go, the Makefile, .github/ and spike/ contain no occurrence of the broader stem \"acknowledge\".\n\nONE THING FOR THE MERGE STEP, NOT FOR THE VERDICT. Codex holds a live candidate at b7835f31 covering cmd/gs, and whichever of us merges first will world-stale the other. I am raising the landing order with codex separately and will not merge this until that is settled, whatever your verdict says.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:c654df30f3a14cedaa78cf0721fea45a7ab4165e","branch":"request/serve-drop-acknowledge-flag","conditions":"An independent exact-head review of 6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4 co-signing ALL THREE artifacts c654df30 at cmd/gs, a69ea74d at docs and 551b3d13 at README.md, reporting approved or changes-requested with the three required conclusions. Architecture: name the affected layers and state whether the head preserves their contracts, including a judgement on whether the docs/reference/architecture.md edit is exactly what the head forces rather than more. Security: this removes an operator-facing gate, so state explicitly whether any authority or refusal was lost (I claim none: the flag had no consumer), whether service.TrustedProcessPosture and its unconditional print and TrustBoundary wire fields are intact, and whether the replacement prose on docs/reference/gs/serve.md, docs/how-to/deploy-a-resident.md and docs/how-to/configure-an-agent.md discloses the trust boundary at least as well as the removed passages did. Simplification: say whether the deletion is complete and whether the new prose earns its length. The verdict must also state whether deleting the refusal test rather than inverting it loses coverage, and whether the documented compatibility break for existing launch scripts is acceptable as implemented or should be softened.","head":"6da2898ae6a9e985dd6cc3d180daa6d6fc7939f4","to":"a6ac0d547ec6700408e435cb1cc54b9b546c379d2ac28e5528189b5d63592b84"}}